Rebase (--rebase)
Takes your local commits, temporarily removes them, pulls in the remote commits, then replays your commits on top as if you wrote them after the remote changes:

D---E---F---G---H---I---A'---B'---C'  (your commits re-applied on top)
Pros: Clean, linear history — easier to read.
Cons: Rewrites your local commit hashes (A becomes A', etc.). This is fine for unpushed commits, but can cause issues if you've already pushed them.


